Answer: The Gill thread performs a finite sequence of practice activities. The batting coach and bowling coach repeatedly provide support. Both coach threads are marked as daemon before start() is called. The exact order of printed messages can vary because the threads execute concurrently.

```java
public class DaemonThreadDemo {    private static void pause() {        try {            Thread.sleep(1000);        } catch (InterruptedException e) {            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();        }    }
static class Gill extends Thread {        @Override        public void run() {            System.out.println("Gill is inspecting the pitch");            pause();            System.out.println("Gill is warming up");            pause();            System.out.println("Gill is doing catching practice");            pause();            System.out.println("Gill is doing batting practice");            pause();            System.out.println("Gill is doing bowling practice");            pause();            System.out.println("Gill has finished and is going to the hotel");        }    }
static class BattingCoach extends Thread {        @Override        public void run() {            while (true) {                System.out.println("Batting coach is assisting Gill");                pause();            }        }    }
static class BowlingCoach extends Thread {        @Override        public void run() {            while (true) {                System.out.println("Bowling coach is assisting Gill");                pause();            }        }    }
public static void main(String[] args) {        Gill gill = new Gill();        BattingCoach battingCoach = new BattingCoach();        BowlingCoach bowlingCoach = new BowlingCoach();        battingCoach.setName("Batting Coach");        bowlingCoach.setName("Bowling Coach");        battingCoach.setDaemon(true);        bowlingCoach.setDaemon(true);        gill.start();        battingCoach.start();        bowlingCoach.start();    }}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output (illustrative; thread scheduling may change the order): Gill is inspecting the pitch Batting coach is assisting Gill Bowling coach is assisting Gill Gill is warming up Batting coach is assisting Gill Gill is doing batting practice Bowling coach is assisting Gill Gill has finished and is going to the hotel

Answer: Create the thread object, call setDaemon(true), and then call start(). Do not call setDaemon(true) after start().

```java
Thread helper = new Thread();
helper.setDaemon(true);
helper.start();
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: No output is required; the snippet demonstrates the required method-call order.
